(-1) Please send me an email within CANVAS if something is not clear.

(0) The following general rules will remain in force unless explicitly note=
d otherwise.



(1) Each exam has a DUE DATE and an AVAILABILITY DATE.  These can be looked=
 up on CANVAS. The Agreement Form, Top Sheets (Page 0) and the Final Grade =
Form also have due dates; submission after the due date does not allow one =
to get free points.



(2) Each student is REQUIRED to submit a COMPLETED EXAM by the deadline on =
the due date. In other words, whatever IS SUBMITTED by that time on that da=
te is defined to be the COMPLETED EXAM, whether so intended or not; there i=
s NO CHOICE.

I suggest VERY STRONGLY that one upload whatever one has finished with the =
top sheet filled in to reflect the contents at least 24 hours prior to the =
deadline on the due date.

One can, thereafter, upload another document with more work done in the las=
t 24 hours before the DEADLINE ON THE DUE DATE, IF NECESSARY, and the more =
recent document will be graded. This simple device will prevent various pro=
blems in case there is any mishap.



(3) A student is REQUIRED to begin the process of uploading 1 HOUR before t=
he 23:59 hours that is by 22:59 hours on the due date and this produces a t=
ime stamp. This is to prevent the type of nonsense that arose frequently du=
ring earlier terms with irresponsible individuals who started uploading wit=
h one minute or less to spare and submitted various real or fictitious expl=
anations for submission failure.  There are time stamps and I will indeed l=
ook at these as will IT.



(3) In case one does start the process AS IN ITEM (2) and something DOES GO=
 wrong with the upload, one is REQUIRED to contact IT with a screen-shot an=
d write to me within CANVAS with the screen-shot and a response from IT. If=
 IT is not contacted (quite irrespective of whether they are open because o=
ne can leave a message and get a time stamp) then there will be no document=
ation to support any claim that there were problems and finding redress may=
 prove impossible.



(4) If one fails to follow the above steps, the grade on the exam will be 0=
 (if page 0 has already been submitted before the deadline on the due date)=
 and if nothing is submitted by the deadline on the due date, the course-gr=
ade will be an F. The reward for non-submission is a grade of F.

The incentive of 7 points to submit the top-sheet )page 0) for every exam a=
nd the top-sheet (page 0) of the problem-set by the deadline is to prevent =
the outcome of a grade of F.



(5) An EMERGENCY is a situation outside one's control that occurs BEFORE 23=
:59 hours on the due date. If an emergency does occur under the constraints=
 described in the above, a student needs to inform me at the EARLIEST OPPOR=
TUNITY with COMPLETE, VERIFIABLE DOCUMENTATION and such situations will be =
dealt with on a case-by-case basis. College policies require such documenta=
tion. If proper, verifiable documentation is not provided the case will be =
referred to the Department Chair and Dean for further investigation. Proble=
ms with scanning and uploading do not constitute emergencies if they are no=
t begun one hour prior to the deadline on the due date, as explained above.=
 One must check one's devices several hours in advance to make sure that th=
ings are working.



(6) In the case of an emergency or in any other situation in which a studen=
t wants to make some COSMETIC CHANGE, the student may re-upload the exam du=
ring the grace period (that is the 24 hours BETWEEN THE DEADLINE ON THE DUE=
 DATE AND THE DEADLINE ON THE AVAILABILITY DATE) and the more recent versio=
n will be graded. I receive an announcement with a time stamp telling me wh=
en the first version was submitted. ONE MUST ALSO SEND ME A LIST OF THE CHA=
NGES MADE BY EMAIL WITHIN CANVAS IMMEDIATELY UPON SUBMITTING SUCH COSMETIC =
CHANGES. Otherwise the previous submission will be graded. One may not atte=
mpt any new problems during the grace period, that is, the time between the=
 deadline on the due date and the deadline on the availability date.



(7) In earlier terms, some irresponsible individuals began to submit blank =
copies of exams before 23:59 hours on the due date and wished to extend the=
 time for which they could work by one day. I have no sympathy for such irr=
esponsibility and will not entertain any discussion regarding the matter. I=
f one engages in such actions against my explicit warnings, they are taking=
 a chance and risk getting 0's and failing the class. It takes a few irresp=
onsible individuals to cause the entire body of students to lose the benefi=
ts of a proper setup. I will deal with such actions with the utmost severit=
y.



(8) A 4-credit class online class requires at least 12 hours of solid work =
per week. If a student is ill-prepared, has sloppy habits, and lacks discip=
line, then it may take more time. Thus for a 4-credit asynchronous online c=
lass, one needs to spend at least 16 (=3D4+12) hours per week on the materi=
al; the above estimate includes the 4 hours that one would have spent in cl=
ass for synchronous or in-person classes. 16 to 20 hours per week is normal=
. 4 hours should be enough to read the lecture notes for an exam carefully.=
 If one does not spend enough time on the material, one can expect to have =
serious problems with the material. During the summer, a course runs at dou=
ble the pace. Hence the time per week is between 32 and 40 hours according =
to the estimate in the above.



