<p>(-1)<span>&nbsp;</span><strong>Please send me an email within CANVAS if =
something is not clear</strong>.</p>
<p><br>
(0)<span>&nbsp;</span><strong>The following general rules will remain in fo=
rce unless explicitly noted otherwise</strong>.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>(1) Each exam has a<span>&nbsp;</span><strong>DUE DATE and an AVAILABILI=
TY DATE.&nbsp; These can be looked up on CANVAS</strong>. The Agreement For=
m, Top Sheets (Page 0) and the Final Grade Form also have due dates; submis=
sion after the due date does not allow one
 to get free points.&nbsp;</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>(2)<span>&nbsp;</span><strong>Each student is REQUIRED to submit a COMPL=
ETED EXAM by the deadline on the due date. In other words, whatever IS SUBM=
ITTED by that time on that date is defined to be the COMPLETED EXAM, whethe=
r so intended or not; there is NO CHOICE.<span>&nbsp;</span></strong></p>
<p><strong><span>I suggest VERY STRONGLY&nbsp;that one upload whatever one =
has finished with the top sheet filled in to reflect the contents&nbsp;at l=
east 24 hours&nbsp;prior to the deadline on the due date.</span></strong></=
p>
<p><strong><span>One can, thereafter, upload another document with more wor=
k done in the last 24 hours before the DEADLINE ON THE DUE DATE, IF NECESSA=
RY, and the more recent document will be graded. This simple device will pr=
event various problems in case there
 is any mishap</span>.</strong></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>(3)<span>&nbsp;</span><strong><span>A student is REQUIRED to begin the p=
rocess of uploading 1 HOUR before the 23:59 hours that is by 22:59 hours on=
 the due date and this produces a time stamp. This is to prevent the type o=
f nonsense that arose frequently during
 earlier terms with irresponsible individuals who started uploading with on=
e minute or less to spare and submitted various real or fictitious explanat=
ions for submission failure.&nbsp; There are time stamps and I will indeed =
look at these as will IT</span>.&nbsp;</strong></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>(3)<span>&nbsp;</span><strong>In case one does start the process AS IN I=
TEM (2) and something DOES GO wrong with the upload, one is REQUIRED to con=
tact IT with a screen-shot and write to me within CANVAS with the screen-sh=
ot and a response from IT</strong>. If
 IT is not contacted (quite irrespective of whether they are open because o=
ne can leave a message and get a time stamp) then there will be no document=
ation to support any claim that there were problems and finding redress may=
 prove impossible.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>(4) I<strong>f one fails to follow the above steps, the grade on the exa=
m will be 0 (if page 0 has already been submitted before the deadline on th=
e due date) and if nothing is submitted by the deadline on the due date,<sp=
an>&nbsp;</span><span>the course-grade
 will be an F</span>.<span>&nbsp;</span><span>The reward for non-submission=
 is a grade of F</span>.</strong></p>
<p><strong>The incentive of 7 points to submit the top-sheet )page 0) for e=
very exam and the top-sheet (page 0) of the problem-set by the deadline is =
to prevent the outcome of a grade of F.&nbsp;</strong></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>(5) An<span>&nbsp;</span><strong>EMERGENCY</strong><span>&nbsp;</span>is=
 a<span>&nbsp;</span><strong>situation outside one's control</strong><span>=
&nbsp;</span>that occurs<span>&nbsp;</span><strong>BEFORE 23:59 hours</stro=
ng><span>&nbsp;</span><strong>on the due date</strong>. If an
 emergency does occur under the constraints described in the above, a stude=
nt needs to inform me at the<span>&nbsp;</span><strong>EARLIEST OPPORTUNITY=
 with COMPLETE, VERIFIABLE DOCUMENTATION</strong><span>&nbsp;</span>and suc=
h situations will be dealt with on a case-by-case
 basis. College policies require such documentation. If proper, verifiable =
documentation is not provided the case will be referred to the Department C=
hair and Dean for further investigation. Problems with scanning and uploadi=
ng do not constitute emergencies
 if they are not begun one hour prior to the deadline on the due date, as e=
xplained above. One must check one's devices several hours in advance to ma=
ke sure that things are working.&nbsp;</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>(6)<span>&nbsp;</span><strong>In the case of an emergency or in any othe=
r situation in which a student wants to make some COSMETIC CHANGE, the stud=
ent may re-upload the exam during the grace period (that is the 24 hours BE=
TWEEN THE DEADLINE ON THE DUE DATE AND
 THE DEADLINE ON THE AVAILABILITY DATE) and the more recent version will be=
 graded. I receive an announcement with a time stamp telling me when the fi=
rst version was submitted.<span>&nbsp;</span><span>ONE MUST ALSO SEND ME A =
LIST OF THE CHANGES MADE BY EMAIL WITHIN
 CANVAS IMMEDIATELY UPON SUBMITTING SUCH COSMETIC CHANGES. Otherwise the pr=
evious submission will be graded.&nbsp;</span><span>One may not attempt any=
 new problems during the grace period, that is, the time between the deadli=
ne on the due date and the deadline on
 the availability date.</span></strong></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>(7)<span>&nbsp;</span><strong>In earlier terms, some irresponsible indiv=
iduals began to submit blank copies of exams before 23:59 hours on the due =
date and wished to extend the time for which they could work by one day. I =
have no sympathy for such irresponsibility
 and will not entertain any discussion regarding the matter. If one engages=
 in such actions against my explicit warnings, they are taking a chance and=
 risk getting 0's and failing the class. It takes a few irresponsible indiv=
iduals to cause the entire body
 of students to lose the benefits of a proper setup. I will deal with such =
actions with the utmost severity</strong>.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>(8) A 4-credit class online class requires at least 12 hours of solid wo=
rk per week. If a student is ill-prepared, has sloppy habits, and lacks dis=
cipline, then it may take more time.&nbsp;<strong>Thus for a 4-credit async=
hronous online class, one needs to spend
 at least 16 (=3D4+12) hours per week on the material; the above estimate i=
ncludes the 4 hours that one would have spent in class for synchronous or i=
n-person classes. 16 to 20 hours per week is normal. 4 hours should be enou=
gh to read the lecture notes for an
 exam carefully. If one does not spend enough time on the material, one can=
 expect to have serious problems with the material. During the summer, a co=
urse runs at double the pace. Hence the time per week is between 32 and 40 =
hours according to the estimate
 in the above.</strong></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>(9) A grade will be given to reflect mastery as shown by performance on =
the exams and the problem-set.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>(10)<span>&nbsp;</span><strong>No circumstance, however unfortunate, con=
stitutes reason for lowering grading standards because that ultimately allo=
ws incompetent individuals to assume positions for which they are not quali=
fied thereby raising the probability
 for widespread harm. I have absolute contempt for any practice that lowers=
 standards and do not regard such practice as a manifestation of sympathy; =
in fact, I consider such practice to be a desecration of the feeling of sym=
pathy which never has the goal of
 causing widespread harm</strong>.</p>
